#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7da9cbe0-e57b-5289-be33-c21af3f68ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Primary Roots of Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0d46b85-e673-5a60-b143-660c6cae3e30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285160} {:answer/id #uuid "569caf71-3ce7-5214-9680-d1d03f38e086" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285161} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f134f1-baa9-549d-a1d9-f1c54243277e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285162} {:answer/id #uuid "21e87d5a-487c-58a8-9650-5cf816c15ca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285163} {:answer/id #uuid "658dd5f8-54f2-59b2-99b3-77874ca7d07c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0d46b85-e673-5a60-b143-660c6cae3e30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285160} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e864228-b889-5614-968b-88d2559e57e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78651eb4-8048-5b92-af07-f86f14c30c25" :question/math-academy-id "q-107495" :question/problem "Find the principal $20$th root of unity." :question/worked-solution "Let $z$ be a $20$th root of unity. This means that \n\n$$\n{z}^{20}=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{20}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/20}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal root corresponds to $k=1,$ and is therefore equal to ${e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/10}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c2c755c-59e0-5048-b20c-507f13fce441" :knowledge-point/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Roots of Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4d7ae5d-d5cc-5a7f-a017-0cc1a272d913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/e11d63b3127cf3a162223ae7fca5e875e02622ec21480edd3fbc6cdddb039eae.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285167} {:answer/id #uuid "5da1037f-012d-5fa3-a8a2-01162162cdb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/9111f21143412d791b0195722da4f6d461444f386efd9baf609c70a4fe709fd8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285168} {:answer/id #uuid "11bdf508-7e23-5e05-9848-754182d26b46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/bae536e1ec12168c413a6387f3106512173a34d92daae9ad6218040e7336c7ad.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285169} {:answer/id #uuid "eed69bc8-4626-5a22-a503-c5fa984364d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/c70d25ac3a5ddb2d6765ef8a22f5e9774c9be06dd909d7079c3a361157881bb5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285170} {:answer/id #uuid "7d187bd0-7aca-5483-b493-1f8ed77da5c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/923d1a1b87833551ae6c451f15a6b5d8bdd75623e8d5c20e86357894bc24683d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eed69bc8-4626-5a22-a503-c5fa984364d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/c70d25ac3a5ddb2d6765ef8a22f5e9774c9be06dd909d7079c3a361157881bb5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285170} :answer-field/id #uuid "79f2cd58-327a-596e-99c0-51624647bc42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d172d3ec-37f8-5fb9-8ea7-85c7f30ed514" :question/math-academy-id "q-162239" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams shows all the $3$rd roots of unity?" :question/worked-solution "Geometrically, the $3$rd roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form an equilateral triangle (regular $3$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/c70d25ac3a5ddb2d6765ef8a22f5e9774c9be06dd909d7079c3a361157881bb5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c2c755c-59e0-5048-b20c-507f13fce441" :knowledge-point/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Roots of Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19915621-0138-5923-be83-52bdd8ac3989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/82682434de1fe7e9c7f086334f0ef7e0a7b2fde3d64efd7234ea10f1fb6c3b4f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285174} {:answer/id #uuid "43683992-a956-57e5-a99e-d2b8a7d3101e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/58013857f5024f78c322059e5ffdbf10943b0479722202c972a51ccda4be3525.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285175} {:answer/id #uuid "0781535a-2dff-5587-ad41-92e404ca3d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/d8a917138beb862f6816fc059847659b1ba11e17d6a7a4906309b021baf1d3e5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285176} {:answer/id #uuid "ca23e92c-b5e4-58ca-91e2-4fd7e3e52267" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/51ac4ad5bbc626c58ddbb6efd8c06bb665ef0849591dd846dd959756ea6e41b6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285177} {:answer/id #uuid "1f1e8548-25d5-5908-b697-5e9e87dda942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/b473ada0488958a7faf410a6cadb0a1b9aedfde1925658a1f635913b1e2507c3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0781535a-2dff-5587-ad41-92e404ca3d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/d8a917138beb862f6816fc059847659b1ba11e17d6a7a4906309b021baf1d3e5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285176} :answer-field/id #uuid "49a80822-caa3-554e-972b-dfc326b58faf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8843b4c7-5e47-597f-9c2c-fd29812c3fb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162245" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams shows all the $9$th roots of unity?" :question/worked-solution "Geometrically, the $9$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form a regular nonagon (regular $9$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/d8a917138beb862f6816fc059847659b1ba11e17d6a7a4906309b021baf1d3e5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8874325-af33-506b-b24d-3a3e387bd88c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Roots of Unity With Certain Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0fc62e4-922d-5c84-968e-7c9aa30f931d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285181} {:answer/id #uuid "6eccb878-10fd-5782-a89d-fe79f2df90c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285182} {:answer/id #uuid "752d9baf-25f3-5f35-89cb-7bf3b494e131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285183} {:answer/id #uuid "f29ffb91-710b-5314-9fe3-81339f39922f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285184} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6a5eec-7bbf-50a6-8ea6-9495bc1ce328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f29ffb91-710b-5314-9fe3-81339f39922f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285184} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fafb208-fdc0-5379-a02a-fd6fd38c13a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ed5b8d5-3a7c-59e2-b811-f15ac112925f" :question/math-academy-id "q-216709" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/bf78e90c844df2d80ffb3b08476ecd0370ffbbf63050bcabf20265692b2deeb3.png)\n\nFind the root of unity highlighted in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince there are $9$ points in our diagram, we need to find a $9$th root of unity.\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{9}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/9}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/e797056434dc122218bdc501cd9e1d5af93127658daa14f7c38b3bd7be8b877f.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=3.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{3}={e}^{2(3)\\pi \\text{i}/9}={e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8874325-af33-506b-b24d-3a3e387bd88c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Roots of Unity With Certain Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd1ff5c8-bc2f-56e2-bba2-a7bb1aab5b97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285188} {:answer/id #uuid "15eea9ed-3453-5a51-83cb-59e8194c203a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285189} {:answer/id #uuid "77fcdca2-2288-5fb4-9e03-fc7848c3fbf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285190} {:answer/id #uuid "fead26a3-a07b-513f-ae78-601a63ca096c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285191} {:answer/id #uuid "fae61e6c-f1ed-5f9c-9242-1f6d3f426d67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77fcdca2-2288-5fb4-9e03-fc7848c3fbf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285190} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8a265cf-7daf-569c-8012-a454831bec59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "653ed88c-debb-5ddb-9d18-ba57a77e805a" :question/math-academy-id "q-216713" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/70665e4b48e324582ff9b1d448580d2e4462cddc7e51ac42a5766bb605d0bf1c.png)\n\nFind the root of unity highlighted in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince there are $8$ points in our diagram, we need to find a $8$th root of unity.\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{8}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/8}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13995561/assets/31a716f5f4abcda0baea7586f732f9009d2cd4821b429d060d985858c61a3b62.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=3.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{3}={e}^{(3)\\pi \\text{i}/4}={e}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
